## Failures

**D123 — P1. `"acme? no, the holdings one"` still archives the wrong company.**
The D116 guard is a **word list tested per clause**. 24 of my 48 exclusion replies still bind the option the founder excluded: 15 via unlisted words (`exclude acme` — note `except|excepting|excluding` are listed but `exclude` is not), 9 via a negator in an adjacent clause. The tell is a punctuation inversion: **`acme - no` dead-ends, `acme, no` binds** — same reply, opposite destructive outcomes, decided by a comma. Traced end-to-end on candidate source to `archive_company`, no LLM in the loop. *Not a regression* (d724d8c did it too) — all six #75 ledger exemplars genuinely close. The failure is the **closure claim**: the clause-scope limit is disclosed only in its benign direction ("acme holdings, no rush" still binds), never in its destructive one.

**D124 — P1. `entityType: 'employee'` is executable and unresolvable at the same time.**
`canonicalKnowsIt` and `displayName` compute *different* typed fallbacks, so the D119 drop never fires for any word-shaped type outside the 22-key map. `employee` is in the executable action map (`endEmploymentPersonIds`) but `canonicalById` is keyed `person|`, so two **real, named, in-context** people render as `the employee (option 1)` / `(option 2)` — the founder ends someone's employment **with no name shown**, and it really executes. Pre-existing at d724d8c, but not covered by the guarantee D119 was written to make.

**D125 — P2.** Four fabrications d724d8c caught now escape (en dash, parenthetical, "without incident", "and no errors occurred"). The disclosed residual is literally accurate but understates its scope — the splitter knows only `[.!?,;]`.

**D126 — TEST GAP.** run15's D119/D120 cases never execute the drop (their helper is the per-option label loop). Removing it, over-broadening it, and weakening it all left run15 at **exit 0, 48 pass**. And **no** committed suite observes the MIXED drop case — mutant C5 survived the entire battery.

